                                What is Mover?
                                --------------

   Mover is a door which allows an AmiExpress sysop to maintain his or her
file areas.  Are you sick and tired of having to cut and paste file
descriptions by hand?  Upset about having to go to the shell and move the new
uploads into the correct directory?  Angry about having to delete some lame
upload and then going in to remove the loser's credit with the user editor?
Mover makes all these tasks simpler.  Mover allows a sysop to delete a file
description or to move a file descriptions between directories.  Mover also
allows the sysop to do local uploads from a directory or to add descriptions
for "off-line" programs.  Mover also allows for description editing and for
moving files from the hold directory.  Mover is SHAREWARE!


                                  Disclaimer
                                  ----------

   Mover reads and writes several of the files used by AmiExpress.  Although
every effort was made to ensure that Mover works correctly, there is always
the possibility that Mover could corrupt your files.  Neither the author nor
those acting as his agents accept responsibility for the use or mis-use of
Mover.  The user accepts full responsibility once Mover is installed.  A
special note of warning:  As Mover must access files which user's on the BBS
can access, care must be taken when user's are logged in to other nodes.
Mover will attempt to handle all file conflicts by displaying a message
telling you the file that is in use and letting you press a key to allow Mover
to try the file again.  Mover was written to work with AmiExpress 2.0+, to
make Mover work with AmiExpress 3.0, please read the section "Making Mover
Work With AmiExpress 3.0."


                               Installing Mover
                               ----------------

   Mover is meant to integrate easily into the AmiExpress environment.  To
install Mover, simply copy it to your DOORS: directory and make the neccessary
changes to your CustomCommands file in your BBS:Commands directory.  For
example, after you have copied Mover to your DOORS: directory, add the
following line to your CustomCommands file:

*MOVER    XS240DOORS:Mover

Please refer to the AmiExpress documentation for setting the security level of
Mover.  As Mover is a Sysop utility, you do not generally want any user to
have access to it.

To take full advantage of Mover's abilities, you should use the Sent by: option
in your file areas.

   This is all that is required to get Mover up and running.  There are some
additional options, so please read on.


                                 Using Mover
                                 -----------

   To use Mover, you must first login in to AmiExpress.  Once logged in, join
the conference you wish to maintain.  Type in the command you have chosen to
activate Mover (If you chose the default configuration, you would type
"Mover").  Once Mover is started, it will tell you which conference you are
in, how many directories are in this conference, and whether or not you have
registered Mover.  If you have not registered Mover, you will only be able to
move or delete one file.  If you have not created a MOVER.CFG file (described
below) you will be given the opportunity to create one.  If you have created
one, you will be given a list of the directories in the current conference.
You may then choose a directory number to maintain, 'S' to perform a sysop
upload, or 'R' to reconfigure your MOVER.CFG file.  Choose a directory number,
'S', or 'R' to continue.

                            File Area Maintainance
                            ----------------------

   Once you have chosen the file area to act upon, Mover will display a list
of up to 26 files, each associated with a letter (A-Z).  The list may look
like:

                  Directory 5
                  New Uploads

A.  assignx.lha                 G.  betrspch.lha
B.  MOD-Chi.lha                 H.  samplez.lzh
C.  Dragon.dms                  I.  modulez.lzh
D.  undim.dms                   J.  qmouse.lha
E.  mod.chi.lha                 K.  runback.lzh
F.  bBaseII.lha


Press <ENTER> to quit, Press (1-6) to choose new directory (0 Quits), or
Choose a file (A-M) :

To perform one of Mover's operations on a file, type the letter associated
with that file and press <ENTER>.  If you want to select a new directory, type
the number of that directory and press <ENTER>.  If you want to quit Mover,
press 0.  The files are always listed in the same order in which they appear
in your directory listings (oldest file at the top, youngest file at the
bottom).  If you have set up a MOVER.CFG file (described below), you will also
see that certain files will show up in red, yellow, or white, depending upon
their age.  If there are more than 26 files in this directory, you can press
<ENTER> to continue listing the files.  If there are no more files in the
current directory, pressing <ENTER> will allow you to choose a new directory.

   Once you have selected a file, Mover will display the full description of
the file and a menu of operations to perform upon the file.  For example:

GTOOLSBX.LHA P 318848  07-14-92  The complete archive of GadToolsBox. Include
                                 s source for the program.  This program
                                 allows the generation of C or assembly code
                                 for WB2.0 Screens, Windows, and Gadgets.
                                 Sent by: Roy Sutton

1> Delete this listing from this directory
2> Move this listing to another directory
3> Edit this description
4> Choose new directory
5> Re-list files

Anything else quits :/>

                            Deleting A Description
                            ----------------------

   If you choose 1, Delete, Mover will delete the description of the file from
the directory listing.  If you have turned on the "Sent by:" option and you
have registered Mover, you will be given the opportunity to take away the
uploader's credit.  If you choose to take away the uploader's credit, both their
files uploaded and bytes uploaded will be altered.  After this choice, another
menu will be displayed.  This menu allows you to do something with the file
associated with the description.  Mover will assume you wish to delete the
file, and place a "1" in the input buffer for you.  If this is what you want
to do, press <ENTER>.  If you wish to move the file to some other directory,
choose 2.  You will then be asked to enter a path (and optionally a new name)
for the file.  If you want to leave the file where it is, press backspace, 3,
then <ENTER>.  Mover will search for the file do be deleted or moved by first
checking the file ULPATH, if it exists.  If you do not have a ULPATH or the
file is not found in one of those paths, Mover will search through your PATHS
file.  If Mover still cannot find the file, an error message will be
displayed.  If the file is in the HOLD directory, Mover will find it there.

                             Moving A Description
                             --------------------

   If you choose 2, Move, Mover will allow you to move the description to
another directory.  If you have set up a MOVER.CFG file, Mover will display a
list of the directories available.  Choose the destination directory.  Mover
will always make sure that the file is inserted in the correct order so that
new scans will work.  After the description has been moved, Mover allows you
to move the file associated with the description.  If you have set up a
MOVER.CFG file, Mover will fill in the input with the path specified in the
MOVER.CFG file.  You can override this choice by backspacing over the input.
If you wish to prevent the move, clear the entire line and press <ENTER>.  If
you have not set up a MOVER.CFG file, type in the destination path at the
prompt.  Mover will search for the file to move in the same manner as
described in the "Deleting A Description" section.

   If you are moving a file from the Hold (Private uploads) directory and you
have registered Mover, Mover will ask you if you wish to credit the uploader
for the upload.  AmiExpress does not credit the user for private uploads,
therefore, you may want to give the user credit for the file when you move it
into a normal file area.  Mover automatically removes the '/' from the
description when moving it to another conference.

                            Editing a Description
                            ---------------------

   If you choose 3, Edit this description, Mover will use your default editor
to edit the file.  Once you have completed modifying the description, Mover
will paste the description back into the directory.  It is important to
remember that file descriptions cannot be more than 44 characters long.

                           Choosing A New Directory
                           ------------------------

   If you choose 4, Choose new directory, Mover will allow you to enter a new
directory number.  If you have set up a MOVER.CFG file, Mover will display a
list of the directories.  Choose a directory and press <ENTER>.

                            Re-Listing A Directory
                            ----------------------

   If you have passed the file you wanted to work on, choose 4, and your
current directory will be listed again from the oldest file.


                                Sysop Uploads
                                -------------

   To perform a sysop upload, choose 'S' from the startup menu.  If you are
maintaining a directory, press '0' and return to return to the startup menu.
Once you have selected 'S', Mover will prompt you to enter the directory from
which it will upload files.  This allows the sysop to place the files to be
uploaded into a hold directory and then select them one by one to be uploaded
to the board and placed in the correct directories.  If you have already placed
the files in the correct directories or you want to do an off-line upload (a
description, but no file attached) press <ENTER> leaving the answer blank.  If
you entered a directory name, Mover will prompt you to enter the file name or
file pattern to be used to search the directory.  Mover will suggest '#?'.
Mover uses the standard AmigaDOS wildcard characters (mainly because they are
more flexible than the ones which AmiExpress allows).  If you know the name of
the file you want to upload, erase the '#?' and type in the name of the file.
Mover will search the directory specified.  If it finds a match, it will ask if
you want to upload the file.  At this point you may answer 'Y'es, 'N'o, or
'A'bort.  If you choose 'Y'es, Mover will extract the information about the file
size from AmigaDOS.  Mover will then ask for a description of the File.  You
may enter as many lines of description as you would like.  To stop entering the
description, press <ENTER> on a blank line.  Mover will then present a list of
destination directories and ask for you to choose one.  Once you have chosen
the destination directory, Mover will prompt for the directory in which to place
the file.  If you would rather not have Mover move the file, clear the response
field and press <ENTER>.  If there are more files that match the search pattern,
they will be displayed.

   If you are performing an off-line upload, Mover will prompt you for the
name of the file you would like to upload.  After you have entered the filename,
Mover will ask for the file size.  Once you have entered this information, Mover
will ask for the destination directory.  Once you have entered this, the file
will be added to the file listings.

   Files uploaded will automatically be placed at the end of the target
directory's file listing.  Mover puts the current date into the date field for
you.  This is a good thing as it allows people to do a new scan off all
directories and see the new files you have added.


                            Setting Up A MOVER.CFG
                            ----------------------

   Beginning with Mover v1.4, Mover has the ability to help you set up a
MOVER.CFG file for each one of your conferences.  If you start Mover and you
have not created a MOVER.CFG file, Mover will ask you if you would like to
create one.  Answer 'Y'es to the prompt.  Mover has the ability to color-code
files based on their age.  Mover will ask you for the maximum number of days
old a file should be before it will be displayed in yellow (files younger than
this number will be displayed in white).  Mover will then ask for the maximum
number of days old a file can get before it is displayed in red.  Files older
than the first number, but younger than the second number will be displayed in
yellow.  If the first number is the same as or greater than the second number,
files will only be displayed as white or red.  Mover will now display the
number of directories in the current conference.  Mover will prompt you for a
name and a default directory for each directory in the conference.  The name of
the directory should probably be the same as the name given in your FileHelp.txt
file.  The default path is simply the path in which you would like Mover to
place files moved from other directories.  If you have an off-line directory,
you may want to set the default path of that directory to NIL: so that files
are automatically deleted when their description is moved to the off-line
directory.

   Mover needs a MOVER.CFG file for each conference.  Mover will create the
MOVER.CFG file and place it in the CONF_LOCAL directory of the current
conference, as specified in your ACP.Startup.  If you decide to change your
MOVER.CFG file in the future, choose the 'R'econfigure option from the startup
menu.

                    Making Mover Work With AmiExpress 3.0
                    -------------------------------------

   Currently, Mover only understands the ULPATHS and PATHS files that Ami-
Express 2.0 uses.  AmiExpress 3.0 has this information stored in an icon's
tooltypes.  For Mover to work effectively with 3.0, you should either not
delete these files when upgrading or create these files with the information
stored in the corresponding icons.

   Current Beta versions of AmiExpress 3.0 have an altered user data file
that is incompatible with Mover.  You should not use the features that modify
a users upload bytes with 3.0.

                              Registering Mover
                              -----------------

   If you have not registered Mover, you will only be able to perform one file
operation before Mover exits back to AmiExpress.  Additionally, you will not
be able to take advantage of the feature which allows modification of the
user's byte and file counts when deleteing or moving a file.  To register
Mover, send $10 to:

         By Mail:                      Electronically:

         Roy Sutton                    R.SUTTON6 on GEnie
         60 SW 91st Ave. #304          rsutton on BIX
         Plantation, FL                rsutton@bix.com on Internet
                    33324-2545         Roy Sutton on (305)475-7785

   When you register Mover, you will need to include the Sysop name and BBS
name EXACTLY as they appear in your ACP.Startup.  You do not have to include
the ANSI escape sequences.  Also include your BBS number, name, Mover version
number, and return mailing address.  If you include this information on disk,
I will use that disk for your key file.  You may also include your favorite
PD software on disk.  European users should include an extra dollar to cover
return shipping costs.

   The preferred method of registration is through a BBS.  Call The Last! Amiga
BBS or The 4 Am Club! and log in.  Once you have been validated, type "reg" at
the main prompt.  On LAB!, you must be in the AmiExpress support conference.
You will be presented with a questionaire asking for the same information I have
listed above.  After you have completed the information and mailed in your
donation, a file will be attached to a message for you.  This file will contain
the key file for Mover, instructions for installing the key file, and any
updates which have occurred to Mover since the version you received.

   By registering Mover, you ensure that there will be updates and other
AmiExpress utilities.  I am always open to suggestions for new features and
programs.  Please see the section at the end for future enhancements.

   Mover updates are routinely posted to LAB! and The 4 Am Club!(see below).  The
latest version of Mover can always be found on these BBSs.


